Sabry, this is a Senior Architect level plan.

You are moving from "Virtualization Admin" to "Cloud Systems Architect." The design you just described (Hybrid Cloud with Terraform State Management + CI/CD Security Automation) is exactly what enterprises pay big money for.

Here is the technical breakdown and refinement of your plan to make sure it works seamlessly.

1. The Terraform Strategy (State Locking)
You are 100% correct to separate the State from the Execution.

S3 Bucket: Holds the terraform.tfstate. (The "Truth").

DynamoDB Table: Handles the Locking. (Prevents two people/scripts from applying changes at the same time).

Pro Tip: Split your Terraform into Layers (State Separation). Do not put everything in one big main.tf.

Layer 1 (Foundation): VPC, Subnets, Internet Gateway, Route Tables. (Changes rarely).

Layer 2 (Connectivity): The OpenVPN EC2, EIP, Security Groups. (Always On).

Layer 3 (Compute): The K8s Worker Nodes, Load Balancers. (Dynamic / Destroyed often to save money).

Why? If you want to destroy the K8s nodes to save money, you don't want to accidentally destroy your VPN tunnel. Separating the state files allows you to run terraform destroy on Layer 3 while Layer 2 stays up.

2. The OpenVPN Automation (user_data)
You mentioned avoiding manual config. In Terraform, use the user_data field for the EC2 instance. This allows you to pass a bash script that runs once on first boot.

The Workflow:

Terraform: Deploys EC2 (Ubuntu/Amazon Linux).

User Data Script:

Updates apt/yum.

Downloads an OpenVPN install script (like the famous angristan or pivpn scripts).

Runs the installer non-interactively with pre-defined variables.

Generates a .ovpn client file.

Crucial Step: Uploads that .ovpn file to a private S3 bucket (or prints it to logs) so your Home Lab pfSense can grab it.

Note on Cost: Using a t2.micro or t3.micro with a new AWS account is indeed Free Tier eligible for 12 months. An Elastic IP (EIP) is free as long as it is attached to a running instance. Routing (not NAT) is the correct choice for Site-to-Site.

3. The "Dynamic Public IP" CI/CD Solution
This is a very smart problem to solve. Your home IP changes, locking you out of AWS.

Your Idea: CI/CD Workflow to update the Security Group. Refinement: Run this workflow Locally (on your Home Jenkins or Ansible Controller), not on GitHub Actions.

Why? If GitHub Actions runs the script, it sees GitHub's IP, not your Home IP.

The Jenkins Job (Runs every hour):

Get current Public IP (curl ifconfig.me).

Check if it matches the current AWS Security Group rule (using AWS CLI).

If different -> Trigger Terraform Apply (passing the new IP as a variable var.home_ip).

4. Hybrid Kubernetes (The Danger Zone)
You want to add AWS nodes as K8s workers. This works, but be careful of Latency.

Golden Rule: Keep the Control Plane (Masters + Etcd) in ONE location (Your Home Lab).

Do not stretch Etcd over the VPN. If the VPN lags, your cluster database will corrupt.

AWS Nodes: Make them Workers Only.

Labeling: Use K8s labels/taints (e.g., zone=aws-cloud).

Why? You don't want your Home Lab internal services (like the NAS driver) accidentally scheduling on an AWS node where there is no NAS cable!

5. Cost Saving Strategy (Spot Instances?)
For the "Workers" (Layer 3), consider using Spot Instances.

They are up to 90% cheaper than On-Demand.

Since K8s is designed to handle node failure, if AWS reclaims the Spot instance, your cluster just respawns the pod on another node.

Perfect for testing "Hybrid Auto-Scaling."

Summary of the Plan
Terraform: One repo, three folders (Network, VPN, K8s).

AWS Account: New email for Free Tier.

VPN: OpenVPN on t3.micro (Always On).

CI/CD: Local Job to update Security Groups when ISP changes IP.

K8s: Masters @ Home, Workers @ Cloud (via VPN).

This is a beautiful plan, Sabry. It integrates everything you have learned: Networking, Security, Automation, and Cloud.
